{
  "id": "Compound::DB00284 - Gene::6476",
  "verbalization": "Acarbose exerts its pharmacological effects by binding to Amy1a and Amy1b, which are isoforms of the enzyme alpha-1,4-glucosidase. These enzymes are involved in the digestion of dietary carbohydrates, and their participation in this process is linked to the target entity Si. The evidence suggests that Acarbose interacts with two different isoforms of the same enzyme, both of which are involved in carbohydrate digestion, and that this interaction is reflected in the participation of Si in the process. The binding of Acarbose to these isoforms likely inhibits their activity, thereby reducing carbohydrate digestion and contributing to the drug’s mechanism of action. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a strong, reliable relationship between Acarbose and Si, mediated through the enzyme alpha-1,4-glucosidase. The mechanism is supported by the shared participation of Si in the digestion of dietary carbohydrates, suggesting a direct and significant interaction between Acarbose and Si.",
